BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Grim History of the Death Penalty in Canada
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T190000
UID:6a63df710e562e28009c3fae@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Death by hanging was a grim reality within the Canadian crimin
 al justice system until its abolition in 1976. But what did capital punish
 ment look like in Canada? In this session\, author Lorna Poplak will prese
 nt the history of the death penalty in Canada\, with a focus on the incons
 istent application of the death sentence\, the treatment of women\, wrongf
 ul conviction\, the difficult journey toward abolition\, and the last two 
 hangings at Toronto’s notorious Don Jail.\nInformation Collection Notice
 :\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publi
 c Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20
 (d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\
 , delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your in
 formation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 41
 6-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Pape/Danforth\, Second floor program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a63df710e562e28009c3fae
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AI for Small Business and Entrepreneurs
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261102T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261102T160000
UID:6a9b51982dac6e0037243871@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:You've got the business idea. Now come the thousand questions:
  who's my customer\, what's the market\, how do I pitch this?\nIn this cla
 ss led by our Innovator-in-Residence\,\nRyan Kelln\n\, you'll use AI as a 
 research and planning partner for small business. We'll practice together 
 on a sample business\, turning rough ideas into business plans\, digging i
 nto markets\, drafting customer-discovery questions\, and building useful 
 low-maintenance automation. The most valuable thing you'll get is usually 
 a better question to be tested in the real world. Whether you're starting 
 or growing\, you'll leave with an experiment you can run on your own busin
 ess this week.\nPrerequisites: Mouse\, keyboarding and internet skills. 
 Computers are provided. No coding experience required.\nRegistration is r
 equired and will start October 9\, 2026 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will
  not be guaranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAfter thi
 s time\, remaining seats will be filled on a first come\, first serve basi
 s until capacity is reached. If you wish to save your work from this hand
 s-on program\, please bring your own USB stick.\nAbout the AI Tools Used i
 n This Program\nThis program includes activities that use agentic AI tools
  (listed below) on library laptops.\nYou do not need to create your own ac
 count for this program.\nParticipants are encouraged to follow the Residen
 t’s guidance throughout sessions\, which include best practices on using
  AI.\nWe encourage you to review the privacy statements of each tool prior
  to attending this program:\nClaude Code\nLearn more about the tool\nRevie
 w privacy policy\nClaude Cowork\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy
