BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6a8212a8aafa6100295d0432@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Come join the readers at Long Branch each month. We share opin
 ions and thoughts of the books selected. For the Month of October take a l
 ook at "Children of the Moon" by Anthony DeSa.\nFrom critically-acclaimed 
 author Anthony De Sa\, a raw and compelling story of love\, war and the di
 fferences that both divide and unite us. In the wispy grasslands of Tanzan
 ia in the 1950s\, a Maasai woman gives birth to a miracle. The other villa
 gers see the baby as a curse. The baby--a daughter\, named Kibo--has skin 
 as white as bone\, and from her first moments on earth\, she is forced to 
 claim her life in spite of the violence she incites. As she grows\, Kibo s
 heds her identity to become Liloe\, and then Po\, continuously trying to a
 djust herself so that she can fit into the world around her. Further south
 \, in Portuguese-controlled Mozambique\, a young boy\, Ezequiel\, has been
  adopted by two missionaries. Zeca\, too\, struggles with a lack of accept
 ance. Not recognized by his Portuguese father's community\, or by his Mako
 nde mother's tribe\, Zeca lives his life on the borders of both worlds. An
 d when civil war erupts in Mozambique\, Zeca must choose what--and who--he
  is willing to leave behind\nNo Registration Required. Drop in.\nAll Welco
 me\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form 
 is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\
 , sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used fo
 r event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions abou
 t how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline s
 taff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto O
 N M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Long Branch\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8212a8aafa6100295d0432
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:DJ At Any Age: Memories in the Mix
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261126T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261126T200000
UID:6aa062565ecd9e1b553241c8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Bring out your inner DJ in this intergenerational program for 
 seniors and adults. Learn basic djing skills and connect with others over 
 a shared love of music. Artist and educator Deejay Vee from At Any Age wil
 l teach you how to use a dj controller and software in a fun and relaxed a
 tmosphere. Working individually or in pairs\, you'll create and exchange p
 laylists of songs that represent meaningful moments in your life.\nNo prio
 r experience needed.\nThis 5-week program series takes place every Thursda
 y from November 12th – December 10th.\nWeeks 1 – 4: Learn DJing skills
 .\nWeek 5: Showcase! Perform your new skills for the group and your friend
 s and family. Refreshments will be provided. Ingredient list will be ava
 ilable at the program.\nInformation Collection Notice\n: The personal info
 rmation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the 
 Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information
  will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evalu
 ation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be dir
 ected to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yon
 ge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8
LOCATION:Maria A. Shchuka\, 1st Floor\, Children's Programming Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa062565ecd9e1b553241c8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Writing Place Workshop with Marc Nair
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T170000
UID:6ac7de91de300e00451920d5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:​This writing workshop revolves around writing place\, where
  participants will write poems or flash fiction about a place that is near
  and dear to them. Participants take an observational narrative approach t
 o writing about place\, employing selected poetry and flash fiction techni
 ques. They will take turns sharing their work while the facilitator\, poet
  Marc Nair\, offers on-the-spot feedback.\nThis event will feature a\n90 m
 inute Workshop:\nIntroduction - facilitator (Marc) will invite participant
 s to name their favorite place and expand on why it is so significant for 
 them.\nFacilitator will begin by sharing a couple of poems that are rooted
  in a sense of place\, exploring how the poems are written in order to off
 er concrete suggestions for participants.\nParticipants then engage in a f
 ree writing exercise to generate content about a particular place. Facilit
 ator will then invite some participants to share and suggest how to shape 
 the content using a 'seed' idea or image from the free writing.\nFacilitat
 or will also talk about flash fiction (100-200 word) stories as an alterna
 tive to the poem. Facilitator will share some examples.\nParticipants will
  write their own poem or flash fiction over the next twenty minutes.\nThey
  will take turns sharing their work while the facilitator offers on the sp
 ot feedback\nMarc Nair is an artist\, educator and cultural entrepreneur f
 rom Singapore who is currently based in Toronto\, Canada.\nRegistration is
  required.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Third-Floor\, Discussion Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac7de91de300e00451920d5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
