SUMMARY:How to Establish Prices for a Craft or Maker Business
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T193000
UID:6a9073c958433600542b8929@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Setting a price for your crafts and handmade items can be a tr
 icky process. Learn about the different factors that you should take into 
 consideration when determining the right price for your work.\nLed by Heat
 her Young\, professor\, Humber College’s Arts Administration programs​
 . She has taught accounting and financial management to diploma and contin
 uing education students and published a self-study guide\, Finance for the
  Arts in Canada.\nRegistration is required.\nThis program is part of Small
  Business Month.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information
  on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public 
 Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will o
 nly be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. 
 Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed t
 o Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Stre
 et\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Dufferin/St.Clair\, Earlscourt room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9073c958433600542b8929
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AI\, Our Community and What Happens Next
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261219T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261219T160000
UID:6a9b51d02dac6e0037243876@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Twelve weeks of making\, testing\, and learning. Now what do w
 e do with it?\nJoin Innovator in Residence\nRyan Kelln\nfor his closing ev
 ent\, as he reflects on the Applied AI residency: the projects people buil
 t\, the unexpected moments that taught us the most\, and the needs we disc
 overed but couldn't solve. We'll tell our stories. Then it's your turn. In
  small groups\, we'll ask: what should we continue\, what should we change
 ? Help turn what we learned into commitments to the future: what this comm
 unity could build and nurture together\, in fruitful and responsible colla
 boration with AI.\nRegistration is required and will start November 9\, 20
 26 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed after 5 minutes of
  the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, remaining seats will be fill
 ed on a first come\, first serve basis until capacity is reached.\nInforma
 tion Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collecte
 d by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 
 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and
  program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the l
 ibrary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nan
 swerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51d02dac6e0037243876
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AI At Home: No Cloud\, No Account
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T200000
UID:6a9b51cb1c197d11325d708b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What if your AI didn't need the internet at all: no subscripti
 on\, no one recording what you type?\nIn this class led by our Innovator-i
 n-Residence\,\nRyan Kelln\n\, you'll see AI models running entirely on one
  computer\, with no connection and no data leaving the room. We'll compare
  a small local model against a powerful cloud model and plain search: what
  each gets right\, what each costs\, and what the privacy trade-offs reall
 y are. You'll leave with a decision sheet for your own hardware\, an insta
 llation recipe\, and an honest look at what "open" really means when it co
 mes to AI.\nRegistration is required and will start November 9\, 2026 at 1
 2:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed after 5 minutes of the st
 art of the program.\nAfter this time\, remaining seats will be filled on a
  first come\, first serve basis until capacity is reached.\nInformation Co
 llection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by th
 e Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\,
  20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and progra
 m registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library 
 handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerlin
 e@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51cb1c197d11325d708b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Peek Inside an AI Assistant: How It Thinks and Works
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261215T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261215T180000
UID:6a9b51c6aafa61002961b2ab@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:You've asked an AI chatbot questions. But there's another kind
  of AI that doesn't just answer: it acts.\nIn this demo-driven talk with o
 ur Innovator-in-Residence\,\nRyan Kelln\n\, we'll look deeply at always-on
  personal AI assistants. The assistant can read your files\, use your apps
 \, and remember what you've told it\, growing more useful over time. The s
 ame powers that let it help you can hurt you if something goes wrong. We'l
 l also open up what happens inside: the hidden instructions\, the tools it
  uses\, what it remembers and forgets.\nThen we'll look at the AI assistan
 t that helped develop this workshop\, and how we shaped our workflow toget
 her\, and why.\nRegistration is required and will start November 9\, 2026 
 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed after 5 minutes of th
 e start of the program.\nAfter this time\, remaining seats will be filled 
 on a first come\, first serve basis until capacity is reached.\nInformatio
 n Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected b
 y the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(
 a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pr
 ogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the libr
 ary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswe
 rline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51c6aafa61002961b2ab
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How Do AIs "Think"? And Why They Get Things Wrong
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261217T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261217T160000
UID:6a9b51cdaafa61002961b2ad@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:How does a machine actually "think"? Explore the history and l
 atest developments of machine learning.\nIn this class led by our Innovato
 r-in-Residence\,\nRyan Kelln\n\, we'll dig into what's really going on ins
 ide AI systems: how they decide what matters in your question\, where thei
 r training data comes from (and who's missing from it)\, and how hidden in
 structions can sneak into content and hijack an AI. Develop an intuitive u
 nderstanding of how AIs "think" without the math. We'll talk about how AIs
  remember\, how they don't and how they are both similar and different to 
 humans. And we'll ask the question underneath it all: is there anyone home
  inside the machine?\nRegistration is required and will start November 9\,
  2026 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed after 5 minutes
  of the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, remaining seats will be f
 illed on a first come\, first serve basis until capacity is reached.\nInfo
 rmation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is colle
 cted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sectio
 ns 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event 
 and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how th
 e library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\
 nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G
 8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51cdaafa61002961b2ad
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Adult Horror Movie Screening: The Visit
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261016T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261016T193000
UID:6a9325ac2dac6e0037229f51@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for spooky season with a screening of The Visit (2015)
  by director M.Night Shyamalan.\nWhen Becca and Tyler are sent to their gr
 andparents' secluded Pennsylvania farm for a weeklong stay\, they quickly 
 discover something is not right with the elderly couple. Faced with strang
 e rules and increasingly frightening behavior\, the children soon realize 
 it will take all their wits to make it home alive.\nRuntime: 95 minutes.
LOCATION:Pape/Danforth\, 2nd Floor\, Program Room.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9325ac2dac6e0037229f51
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Adult Horror Movie Screening: The Sixth Sense
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261030T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261030T194500
UID:6a932b6c3b6c71003e5c470b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for spooky season with a screening of The Sixth Sense
  (1999) by director M. Night Shyamalan.\nYoung Cole Sear (Haley Joel Osm
 ent) is haunted by a dark secret: he is visited by ghosts. Cole is frighte
 ned by visitations from those with unresolved problems who appear from the
  shadows. He is too afraid to tell anyone about his anguish\, except child
  psychologist Dr. Malcolm Crowe (Bruce Willis). As Dr. Crowe tries to unco
 ver the truth about Cole's supernatural abilities\, the consequences for c
 lient and therapist are a jolt that awakens them both to something unexpla
 inable.\nRuntime: 107 minutes.
LOCATION:Pape/Danforth\, 2nd Floor\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a932b6c3b6c71003e5c470b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Iron Heel of Ruthlessness: Canada’s Hysterical Overreaction 
 to Left-Wing Radicalism a Century Ago
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261112T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261112T193000
UID:6a947bed29a7dd0d6747cf7a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The 1920s and 30s marked the rise of left-wing radicalism in C
 anada and a brutal crackdown from the powers that be. Following the defeat
  of the Kaiser\, communism had become the new menace to inspire fear and l
 oathing across the nation. This presentation by local author and journali
 st\, Nate Hendley\, examines the overreaction to the rise of left-wing rad
 icalism during that time with a focus on specific incidents within Toronto
 .\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form i
 s collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\,
  sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for
  event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about
  how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline st
 aff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON
  M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Pape/Danforth\, Second floor program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a947bed29a7dd0d6747cf7a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
